## The prelude

`skoro::prelude` re-exports what most model-building code needs: the backends, `ModelClient`, the
typed handles `Q`, the manifolds, `Normal`, the binding roles, and the readers. This is the
smallest complete program, quoted from the umbrella crate's doctest:

```rust
{../../../../crates/skoro/src/lib.rs:30..37}
```

A few things to notice:

- `B` is the backend. `Flex` is the CPU backend; everything is generic over it.
- `add_parameter(Real)` returns a `Q<B, Real>`, which is a *handle* to a node rather than a number.
  The quantity has no value until one is supplied at evaluation time.
- `a + b` doesn't add numbers. It adds a *mapped* node to the graph and returns its handle.

## A Gaussian, scored

The next example, from `skoro-normal`'s tests, adds a three-dimensional Gaussian quantity with a
constant mean and a diagonal covariance factor. It then evaluates the model's log-density at the
mean:

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-normal/tests/facade.rs:36..60}
```

This one test shows the whole loop:

1. Quantities are added through `add_quantity(distribution)`, which returns a typed handle.
2. Values are supplied at evaluation time as a map from node id to tensor. Tensors are always
   batched: this one has shape `[1, 3]`.
3. `log_density` returns one log-density per batch row.

